        <!-- SECTION 5: Transfer Switches and Safe Connection -->
        <div id="transfer-switch" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Transfer Switches and Safe Connection
          </h2>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Never Backfeed Through a Dryer Outlet</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Connecting a generator to your home wiring through a double-male cord energizes the utility lines outside your house and can kill line workers repairing the outage. It also bypasses every overcurrent protection in your panel. It is illegal in most jurisdictions and it is the single most dangerous thing homeowners do with generators. Install a transfer switch or use extension cords — there is no third option.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Manual Transfer Switch</h3>
          <p>
            A small sub-panel installed beside your main panel with a set number of circuits — commonly 6, 8, or 10 — that you choose during installation. During an outage you plug the generator into an exterior inlet box, start it, and flip each circuit from utility to generator. Typically $500-1,500 installed including the inlet. This is the standard solution for a portable generator and transforms its usefulness.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Interlock Kit</h3>
          <p>
            A cheaper alternative: a mechanical plate on your existing panel that physically prevents the main breaker and a generator backfeed breaker from being on simultaneously. It gives you access to every circuit in the panel rather than a fixed subset, usually for $200-500 installed. Availability depends on your panel brand and local code, so check with an electrician.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Extension Cords Done Right</h3>
          <p>
            If a transfer switch is not in the budget, use heavy-gauge outdoor-rated cords — 12 gauge for runs up to 50 feet, 10 gauge beyond that — run through a window with a sealed cord pass rather than a door left ajar. Never daisy-chain cords, and never run them under rugs or through standing water.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Cold Weather Operation: Where Most Outages Happen -->
        <div id="cold-weather" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Cold Weather Operation: Where Most Outages Happen
          </h2>

          <p>
            Winter storms cause a disproportionate share of extended power outages, which makes cold-weather behaviour the most important real-world characteristic of a backup generator — and the one least reflected in the specifications.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Propane Vaporisation Problem</h3>
          <p>
            Propane has to boil inside the tank to feed the engine as a gas, and that boiling slows dramatically as temperature falls. Around 20°F a 20-pound tank can no longer vaporise fast enough to supply a large generator at load, and the symptom is distinctive: the unit starts normally, runs for a while, then begins surging or dropping load as the tank frosts over and pressure sags. Owners often mistake this for a fault in the generator.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are three fixes, in order of practicality. Keep gasoline available and switch to it when temperatures drop — the simplest answer, and the reason dual fuel beats propane-only for winter. Use a larger tank, since a 100-pound cylinder has far more liquid surface area to vaporise from and keeps up in much colder conditions. Or connect two 20-pound tanks through a manifold, which splits the demand and roughly halves the vaporisation load on each.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cold Starting on Gasoline</h3>
          <p>
            Gasoline is the more reliable cold-weather fuel, but a cold engine still asks more of the starting system. Electric start depends on a battery whose cranking capacity falls sharply in the cold, which is why a battery that seemed fine in October can be inadequate in January. Keep the battery charged with a maintainer if the generator is stored somewhere unheated, and confirm that the recoil backup actually works — many owners have never pulled it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Oil viscosity matters too. Straight 30-weight oil, which many small engines specify by default, becomes noticeably thick below freezing and makes cranking harder. Most manufacturers approve 5W-30 synthetic for cold-weather operation, which flows far better on a cold start and protects just as well once warm. Check your manual and make the switch before winter rather than during it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Running in Snow</h3>
          <p>
            A generator still needs the full 20-foot setback in a blizzard, which means it will be sitting in the weather. Use a purpose-built generator canopy that maintains airflow and exhaust clearance — never a tarp draped over the machine, which traps exhaust heat against the housing and is a genuine fire risk. Keep the unit elevated on blocks or a platform so accumulating or drifting snow does not reach the air intake, and clear snow away from the exhaust regularly so it cannot pack in and back-pressure the engine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Generator Safety -->
        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Generator Safety
          </h2>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Carbon Monoxide Applies on Propane Too</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Propane burns cleaner than gasoline but still produces carbon monoxide in lethal quantities. Never run any of these generators in a garage, breezeway, covered porch, or within 20 feet of a window, door, or vent — regardless of fuel, and regardless of whether the unit has a CO shutoff sensor. Install battery-powered CO detectors inside the house as a second layer.
            </p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Three more practices matter in real use. Never refuel a hot or running engine with gasoline — spilled fuel on hot exhaust is a genuine fire risk and one of the most common generator injuries. Keep the unit dry and elevated in wet weather using a proper generator canopy rather than a tarp, which traps exhaust heat. And check the propane hose and regulator annually for cracking, soap-testing every connection when you attach a tank.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Exercise It Monthly, on Propane</h3>
          <p>
            The most common failure mode for a stored generator is a carburetor gummed by stale gasoline. Running the unit under load for 20-30 minutes every month keeps everything moving, and doing those runs on propane means no varnish is deposited at all. This one habit prevents most no-start failures.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Oil Changes Matter More Than You Think</h3>
          <p>
            These engines hold roughly a quart, so oil degrades faster than in a car and a missed change matters more. Change after the first 20-25 hours, then every 50-100 hours of run time. Check the level before every extended run, not just at intervals — most of these units have a low-oil shutoff, and finding out it works during an outage is the wrong time.
          </p>
        </div>

