            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much power do you lose running a generator on propane?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                About 10%, consistently. A generator rated 9,200 running watts on gasoline typically delivers around 8,300 on propane, because propane carries less energy per unit volume delivered to the cylinder. If propane will be your primary fuel, size against the propane rating rather than the headline gasoline number.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a dual fuel generator worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For emergency backup, yes — for availability rather than economy. After a regional storm, gas stations without power cannot pump while propane tanks stay on shelves. Propane also stores indefinitely, so several tanks in the garage represent days of power that never degrades, unlike gasoline which goes stale in three to six months.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a dual fuel generator run on a 20 lb propane tank?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a 5,000-9,000 watt unit, typically 5-9 hours at moderate load. Gasoline usually gives longer runtime per fill — 10-14 hours for these machines at half load — but a propane tank can be swapped in seconds without waiting for the engine to cool, which is a real advantage during a long outage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do dual fuel generators work in cold weather on propane?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They struggle below about 20°F. Propane must vaporize inside the tank to feed the engine, and vaporization slows sharply in cold, causing surging or load loss. Keep gasoline available for winter outages, or use a 100 lb tank, which has much more surface area for vaporization than a 20 lb bottle.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I connect a dual fuel generator to my house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only through a manual transfer switch or an approved interlock kit, installed by an electrician. Never backfeed through a dryer outlet with a double-male cord — it energizes the utility line outside your home, can kill line workers, bypasses your panel's overcurrent protection, and is illegal in most jurisdictions.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size dual fuel generator do I need for a house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For essentials — refrigerator, freezer, lights, devices — 3,500-4,500 running watts. Adding a sump pump and a window air conditioner needs 5,500-7,000. Covering most essential circuits through a transfer switch takes 8,000-9,500. A well pump plus central air needs 10,000-12,000 watts.
