        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes With Dual Fuel Generators
          </h2>

          <p>
            These machines are simple to run and easy to ruin in storage. Most failures are preventable.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Backfeeding Through a Dryer Outlet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A double-male cord from the generator to a household outlet energizes the utility lines outside your house and can kill the crew repairing the outage. It also bypasses every breaker in your panel. Install a transfer switch or an interlock kit, or use extension cords directly to appliances.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing Against Gasoline While Planning to Run Propane</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The 10% propane derate lands on surge capacity, which is exactly what starts a well pump or a compressor. If propane is your main fuel, size against the propane rating so the generator still has the headroom you bought it for.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing It With Untreated Gasoline</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A gummed carburetor is the number one reason a stored generator will not start during an outage. Either stabilize the fuel and run it through the carburetor, or shut the fuel valve and let the bowl run dry — then exercise the unit on propane monthly so no varnish forms at all.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Propane Means No Carbon Monoxide</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Propane burns cleaner but still produces CO in lethal amounts. The 20-foot setback from windows, doors, and vents applies identically on propane, and a garage with the door open is not outdoors. CO shutoff sensors are a backup, not a permission slip.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Relying on Propane Alone Through Winter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Below roughly 20°F a 20 lb tank cannot vaporize fast enough to feed a large generator, and it surges or quits — during exactly the storms that cause outages. Keep gasoline on hand for cold weather or step up to a 100 lb tank.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

